\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Extrapolation to held-out extreme poses (7 train / 3 test). Each model is evaluated in its native metric: 2D pixel error for Soloff (3D$\to $2D mapping), 3D triangulation accuracy for Zernike (2D$\to $3D mapping). The degradation ratio (test / train) is the comparable quantity across model families.}}{21}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:extrapolation}{{6}{21}{Extrapolation to held-out extreme poses (7 train / 3 test). Each model is evaluated in its native metric: 2D pixel error for Soloff (3D$\to $2D mapping), 3D triangulation accuracy for Zernike (2D$\to $3D mapping). The degradation ratio (test / train) is the comparable quantity across model families}{table.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces 3-D reconstruction RMS (micrometres) on held-out extreme poses, with both models expressed against a shared board-pose reference. Two symmetric protocols bracket the reference dependence: poses frozen to the CMO model, or to the free-fit Zernike field. A single global rigid (Kabsch) transform, estimated on the training frames, removes the camera-to-world gauge. The qualitative ranking is invariant to the reference choice.}}{22}{table.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:extrapolation_3d}{{7}{22}{3-D reconstruction RMS (micrometres) on held-out extreme poses, with both models expressed against a shared board-pose reference. Two symmetric protocols bracket the reference dependence: poses frozen to the CMO model, or to the free-fit Zernike field. A single global rigid (Kabsch) transform, estimated on the training frames, removes the camera-to-world gauge. The qualitative ranking is invariant to the reference choice}{table.caption.15}{}}
